(3. Results) Tj
0 -16 Td
/F1 11 Tf
(Mean notification density during the reading block was 9.6 events per hour in week one) Tj
0 -13 Td
(\(standard deviation 6.1\). The distribution had a long right tail; a software support pod) Tj
0 -13 Td
(averaged 22 events per hour.) Tj
0 -13 Td
( ) Tj
0 -8 Td
(We found that delayed recall fell as density rose. In a linear model with controls for sleep) Tj
0 -13 Td
(duration, seniority, and topic interest, ten extra non-urgent notifications per hour) Tj
0 -13 Td
(predicted an 18 percent relative drop in quiz score \(95 percent interval 12 to 24 percent\).) Tj
0 -13 Td
(People in the top density quartile recalled 41 percent of items. People in the bottom) Tj
0 -13 Td
(quartile recalled 63 percent.) Tj
0 -13 Td
( ) Tj
0 -8 Td
(Numbers were hit hardest. Participants could still paraphrase the political argument after a) Tj
0 -13 Td
(noisy reading session, but they lost the figures: the $4.50 toll, the 14 percent traffic) Tj
0 -13 Td
(drop in the cited pilot, the three exemptions. That pattern is consistent with a story about) Tj
0 -13 Td
(interrupted encoding of specifics rather than a story about total disengagement.) Tj
0 -13 Td
( ) Tj
0 -8 Td
(The quiet hour cut measured density by 72 percent on average. Quiz scores in quiet-hour) Tj
0 -13 Td
(firms rose 11 points on a 100-point scale relative to their own week-one baseline, while) Tj
0 -13 Td
(waitlisted firms moved less than two points. We cannot call this a fully randomized field) Tj
0 -13 Td
(experiment, but the before-after contrast is hard to ignore.) Tj
0 -13 Td
( ) Tj
0 -8 Td
(Self-report lied in a familiar way. Eighty-one percent of high-density readers said they had) Tj
0 -13 Td
(read the paper carefully. Their quiz scores said otherwise. Managers who glanced at presence) Tj
0 -13 Td
(indicators would have concluded the work was done.) Tj
